        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Renal medulla</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69220,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The typical presentation is that of an abdominal mass. A few patients have presented with manifestations of extrarenal vasculopathy such as hypertension or haemorrhage. The mean age at diagnosis is 24&nbsp;months. Rare adult cases have been described {12352423; 22832110}, as has a case in a patient with neurofibromatosis type&nbsp;1 {19875383}. See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/36/50/20663\" target=\"_blank\">#20663</a>Table&nbsp;2.07 .</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69221,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metanephric stromal tumour is approximately one-tenth as common as congenital mesoblastic nephroma {10895814}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69222,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69223,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most metanephric stromal tumours harbour <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations {26796506}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69224,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metanephric stromal tumours are typically tan, lobulated fibrous masses centred in the renal medulla. The mean diameter is 50&nbsp;mm. Approximately half of cases are grossly cystic, and one&nbsp;sixth are multifocal {10895814}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69225,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "<p>Metanephric stromal tumours are subtly infiltrative neoplasms composed of spindle-shaped to stellate cells with thin, hyperchromatic nuclei. Epithelioid stromal cells are not rare but typically are focal. Metanephric stromal tumours characteristically surround and entrap renal tubules and blood vessels to form concentric (onion-skin) rings or collarettes around these structures in a myxoid background. Most neoplasms induce angiodysplasia of entrapped arterioles, which involves epithelioid transformation of medial smooth muscle and myxoid change. One&nbsp;quarter of metanephric stromal tumours induce juxtaglomerular cell hyperplasia within entrapped glomeruli, which may occasionally lead to hypertension associated with hyperreninism. One&nbsp;fifth of metanephric stromal tumours demonstrate heterologous differentiation in the form of glia or cartilage. Most metanephric stromal tumours are mitotically inactive, but cellular foci may have multiple mitotic figures in a given high-power field. Although metanephric stromal tumours may involve the renal sinus and nerves, as well as undermine the renal pelvic urothelium (suburothelial pads), vascular invasion is absent {10895814}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Metanephric stromal tumours are typically immunoreactive for CD34. Desmin, cytokeratins, PAX8, and S100 are negative, although heterologous glial areas label for GFAP and S100.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-26T15:31:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69226,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69227,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most metanephric stromal tumours harbour <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations {26796506}. This mutation is not found in clear cell sarcoma of the kidney, congenital mesoblastic nephroma, or ossifying renal tumour of infancy, so its presence may support the diagnosis of metanephric stromal tumour in difficult cases {26796506}. Unlike cellular congenital mesoblastic nephromas, metanephric stromal tumours lack the <em>ETV6</em>::<em>NTRK3</em> gene fusion {10658907}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69228,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 50,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a spindle cell neoplasm that shows concentric peritubular growth.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations; angiodysplasia; juxtaglomerular cell hyperplasia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T16:44:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
